# 6) equals() and hashCode() contract violation

### Basic Rule:

Agar:

```java
a.equals(b) == true
```

to:

```java
a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()
```

hona **mandatory** hai.

### Example:

```java
class Employee {
    int id;

    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object obj) {
        Employee e = (Employee) obj;
        return this.id == e.id;
    }

    // hashCode() override nahi kiya
}
```

Suppose:

```java
Employee e1 = new Employee(1);
Employee e2 = new Employee(1);
```

`equals()` true ho sakta hai, but dono ka hashCode different ho sakta hai.

HashMap/HashSet me problem aa sakti hai.

### Example:

```java
Set<Employee> set = new HashSet<>();

set.add(e1);
set.add(e2);
```

Expected: one object because IDs same.

But wrong `hashCode()` ki wajah se dono objects store ho sakte hain.

### Interview line:

> **If two objects are equal according to equals(), they must have the same hashCode(). Otherwise HashMap and HashSet can behave incorrectly.**

**Remember:**

> `equals true → hashCode same`

