# Q21. How can a Java application have a Memory Leak even when Garbage Collector is there?

Sabse pehle simple meaning:

> **Memory Leak ka matlab hai: object ki ab zarurat nahi hai, lekin uska reference abhi bhi kisi jagah stored hai. Isliye Garbage Collector us object ko delete nahi kar pata.**

### Simple Example

```java
class Employee {
    String name;

    Employee(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }
}
```

Ab ek static list hai:

```java
class EmployeeManager {

    static List<Employee> employees = new ArrayList<>();

    public static void addEmployee() {
        Employee e = new Employee("Vishnu");
        employees.add(e);
    }
}
```

Ab baar-baar:

```java
EmployeeManager.addEmployee();
```

call hota raha.

Memory me:

```text
employees
   ↓
Employee 1
Employee 2
Employee 3
Employee 4
Employee 5
...
```

Ab maan lo Employee objects ki zarurat nahi hai.

Lekin:

```java
employees
```

abhi bhi un objects ko reference kar rahi hai.

Isliye GC bolega:

> "Ye objects abhi reachable hain, main inko delete nahi karunga."

### Result:

```text
Objects keep increasing
        ↓
Heap memory keeps increasing
        ↓
Memory pressure
        ↓
OutOfMemoryError ❌
```

---

## GC hone ke baad bhi leak kyun?

Ye sabse important point hai:

**GC unreachable objects ko clean karta hai.**

```text
Object
  ↓
No reference
  ↓
GC can remove ✅
```

But:

```text
Object
  ↑
Reference still exists
  ↓
GC cannot remove ❌
```

---

## Common causes

### 1. Static Collection

```java
static List<Object> list = new ArrayList<>();
```

### 2. Unclosed resources

Jaise database connection, file, stream etc.

### 3. List/Map me unnecessary objects store karte rehna

```java
cache.put(key, object);
```

Aur kabhi remove nahi karna.

### 4. Listeners / callbacks unregister na karna

---

## 🧠 Real-life example

Socho tumhare room me purane boxes hain.

Tum unhe use nahi karte, **but register me unka naam abhi bhi active hai**.

Cleaning person dekhta hai:

> "Ye registered hai, isko nahi hatao."

Exactly waise hi:

```text
Unused Object
      +
Reference still exists
      ↓
GC cannot remove
      ↓
Memory Leak
```

### 🎯 Interview me directly bolo:

> **A memory leak can occur when objects are no longer needed but are still strongly referenced by something like a static collection, cache, or listener. Since the objects are still reachable, Garbage Collector does not remove them, causing memory usage to increase.**

### 🧠 One-line trick:

> **GC removes unreachable objects, not unused-but-still-referenced objects.**

