# Q25. What is a Record in Java? How is it different from a normal class?

### Simple meaning:

**Record** Java me mainly **data ko store karne ke liye** use hota hai.

Agar hume ek simple class banani hai jisme mostly data hi hai, to `record` use kar sakte hain.

### Normal class:

```java
class Employee {

    private final int id;
    private final String name;

    public Employee(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public int getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}
```

Kaafi boilerplate code hai. 😐

---

### Record:

Same thing:

```java
record Employee(int id, String name) {
}
```

Bas! 😎

Java automatically important methods provide karta hai:

```text
constructor
accessor methods
equals()
hashCode()
toString()
```

Record ke accessor methods:

```java
Employee e = new Employee(1, "Vishnu");

System.out.println(e.id());
System.out.println(e.name());
```

Normal JavaBean me generally:

```java
e.getId();
e.getName();
```

Record me:

```java
e.id();
e.name();
```

---

## Important: Record immutable hota hai

```java
record Employee(int id, String name) {
}
```

Iske components ko directly change nahi kar sakte:

```java
e.name = "Rahul"; // ❌
```

Record ka reference bhi final-style semantics follow karta hai for its components.

---

# Record vs Normal Class

| Record                                             | Normal Class                     |
| -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| Mainly data carrier ke liye                        | Any type of object ke liye       |
| Very less code                                     | More boilerplate possible        |
| Components final                                   | Fields mutable bhi ho sakte hain |
| `equals/hashCode/toString` automatically generated | Usually manually/IDE generated   |
| `e.name()`                                         | Usually `e.getName()`            |
| `extends` another class nahi kar sakta             | Class extend kar sakti hai       |

### Example use case:

DTO:

```java
record EmployeeDTO(
    Long id,
    String name,
    String department
) {}
```

Spring Boot application me simple request/response data ke liye record useful hai.

---

### 🎯 Interview me directly bolo:

> **A record is a special Java type designed mainly for immutable data carriers. It reduces boilerplate by automatically providing a constructor, accessors, equals, hashCode and toString.**

### 🧠 Easy trick:

> **Record = Data store karna hai + boilerplate kam chahiye.**

```text
Normal Class → More code
Record       → Less code + Data carrier
```

