# Q1. Difference between `@RequestScope`, `@SessionScope` and `@ApplicationScope`

Ye **Spring Bean Scopes** ka question hai.

Sabse pehle simple meaning:

> **Scope decide karta hai ki Spring Bean ka object kitne time tak aur kis level par same rahega.**

---

## 1. `@RequestScope`

```java
@Component
@RequestScope
public class UserRequestData {
}
```

Iska matlab:

> **Har HTTP request ke liye new object banega.**

Example:

```text
Request 1 → Object A
Request 2 → Object B
Request 3 → Object C
```

Matlab **one request = one object**.

### Use case:

Current request ka temporary data:

```text
Request ID
User request data
Temporary processing data
```

---

# 2. `@SessionScope`

```java
@Component
@SessionScope
public class UserSessionData {
}
```

Iska matlab:

> **Ek user session ke liye same object use hoga.**

Example:

```text
User Login
    ↓
Session starts
    ↓
Object A
    ↓
Request 1 → Object A
Request 2 → Object A
Request 3 → Object A
    ↓
Session ends
```

Session khatam hone ke baad object bhi session ke saath associated nahi rahega.

### Use case:

User-specific session data:

```text
Logged-in user information
Shopping cart
Session preferences
```

---

# 3. `@ApplicationScope`

```java
@Component
@ApplicationScope
public class ApplicationData {
}
```

Iska matlab:

> **Poore Spring application ke liye generally ek shared bean instance hota hai.**

Example:

```text
Application
     |
     ↓
 Object A
  ↙  ↓  ↘
Request1 Request2 Request3
```

Multiple users ke requests same application-scoped bean ko share kar sakte hain.

### Use case:

Application-level shared data/configuration type state.

---

# 🔥 Teeno ek saath

```text
@RequestScope
     ↓
One HTTP Request
     ↓
New Object


@SessionScope
     ↓
One User Session
     ↓
Same Object


@ApplicationScope
     ↓
Whole Application
     ↓
Shared Object
```

### Easy table

| Scope               | Object lifetime               | Simple example             |
| ------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------- |
| `@RequestScope`     | One HTTP request              | Request data               |
| `@SessionScope`     | One user session              | Shopping cart/session data |
| `@ApplicationScope` | Application context/lifecycle | Application-wide data      |

---

## 🎯 Karat me directly bolo:

> **Request scope creates a bean instance for each HTTP request. Session scope creates an instance for each user HTTP session and reuses it across requests in that session. Application scope provides an application-level bean that is shared within the application context.**

### 🧠 Super easy trick:

**Request → Request ke liye**

**Session → User ke session ke liye**

**Application → Puri application ke liye**

---

### ⚠️ Potential impact

Agar galat scope use kiya:

* `RequestScope` → har request me new object, unnecessary object creation ho sakta hai.
* `SessionScope` → user-specific data unnecessarily long time memory me reh sakta hai.
* `ApplicationScope` → shared state hone ki wajah se **thread-safety** ka dhyan rakhna padta hai.

