## Q9. Spring Boot application me logging kaise implement karoge?

Spring Boot me normally **SLF4J + Logback** use hota hai.

### Simple example

```java
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

@Service
public class UserService {

    private static final Logger log =
            LoggerFactory.getLogger(UserService.class);

    public void getUser() {

        log.info("Fetching user");

        try {
            // business logic
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.error("Error while fetching user", e);
        }
    }
}
```

### Log levels

```text
TRACE → Very detailed
DEBUG → Debugging information
INFO  → Normal application information
WARN  → Something unusual
ERROR → Error/Failure
```

Example:

```java
log.info("User created: {}", userId);

log.warn("Payment taking longer than expected");

log.error("Payment failed", exception);
```

---

## Production me kya dhyan rakhen?

### ❌ Password log nahi karna

```java
log.info("Password: {}", password); // ❌
```

### ❌ Sensitive information log nahi karni

Jaise:

```text
Password
API keys
JWT tokens
Credit card details
```

### ✅ Correlation/Trace ID useful hai

Microservices me request:

```text
API Gateway
   ↓
User Service
   ↓
Order Service
   ↓
Payment Service
```

Agar same request ka **trace/correlation ID** logs me ho, to complete request ko trace karna easy hota hai.

---

### Centralized logging

Microservices me har service ke logs ko ek central system me collect kar sakte hain:

```text
User Service ──┐
Order Service ─┼──→ Logstash → Elasticsearch → Kibana
Payment Service┘
```

Tumne ELK use kiya hai, to ye example interview me confidently bol sakte ho.

---

### 🎯 Karat me directly bolo:

> **In Spring Boot, I would use SLF4J with Logback for application logging. I would use appropriate log levels such as INFO, DEBUG, WARN and ERROR. In production, I would avoid logging sensitive information and use correlation IDs for tracing requests across microservices. Logs can be centralized using an ELK stack for searching and monitoring.**

### 🧠 Easy trick:

**Logging = SLF4J + Logback + Levels + No Sensitive Data + Centralized Logs**

