# Java Scenario Q2 — `Cash` class me `deposit()` method aur Custom Exception

Image me question ka meaning hai:

> **Cash class me `deposit()` method kuch conditions check karta hai aur invalid condition me `null` return karta hai. Is design ko improve karke Custom Defined Exception throw karni hai.**

### Pehle problem samjho

Maan lo code aisa hai:

```java
public Cash deposit(double amount) {

    if (amount <= 0) {
        return null;
    }

    // deposit logic
    return this;
}
```

Problem ye hai ki caller ko `null` mil raha hai:

```java
Cash cash = account.deposit(-100);

cash.getBalance();  // NullPointerException ❌
```

`null` return karne ke bajay **meaningful exception** throw karna better hai.

---

# Step 1: Custom Exception banao

```java
public class InvalidDepositException extends RuntimeException {

    public InvalidDepositException(String message) {
        super(message);
    }
}
```

Ab hamari application ka specific exception ready hai.

---

# Step 2: `deposit()` me exception throw karo

```java
public Cash deposit(double amount) {

    if (amount <= 0) {
        throw new InvalidDepositException(
            "Deposit amount must be greater than zero"
        );
    }

    // deposit logic

    return this;
}
```

Ab:

```java
cash.deposit(-100);
```

karne par:

```text
InvalidDepositException
        ↓
"Deposit amount must be greater than zero"
```

---

# Agar multiple conditions hain

Suppose:

```java
public Cash deposit(double amount) {

    if (amount <= 0) {
        throw new InvalidDepositException(
            "Amount must be greater than zero"
        );
    }

    if (amount > 100000) {
        throw new InvalidDepositException(
            "Maximum deposit limit is 100000"
        );
    }

    // deposit
    return this;
}
```

Ab invalid input ko `null` ke through hide nahi kar rahe.

---

# Why Exception is better than `null`?

### `null`

```text
Invalid input
    ↓
return null
    ↓
Caller may not know why
    ↓
Later NullPointerException
```

### Custom Exception

```text
Invalid input
    ↓
throw InvalidDepositException
    ↓
Clear error
    ↓
Caller can handle it
```

---

## 🎯 Karat me directly bolo:

> **Instead of returning null for an invalid deposit, I would create a meaningful custom exception such as InvalidDepositException and throw it when the business condition is violated. This makes the error explicit and prevents unexpected NullPointerExceptions.**

### 🧠 Easy trick:

> **Invalid business condition → Custom Exception, not `null`.**

### Ek important point

Agar interviewer pooche **checked ya unchecked custom exception?**

Business validation ke liye commonly:

```java
extends RuntimeException
```

use kar sakte hain, especially jab caller ko har jagah `try-catch` force nahi karna ho.

