# 2. Unnecessary Beans avoid karo

Agar koi class Spring Bean ki zarurat nahi hai, to unnecessarily:

```java
@Component
```

mat lagao.

Example:

```java
@Component
public class UtilityClass {
}
```

Agar is class ko Spring lifecycle/dependency injection ki requirement hi nahi hai, to normal utility class rakh sakte hain.

---

# 3. `@Lazy` use kar sakte hain

Agar koi bean startup par immediately required nahi hai:

```java
@Lazy
@Service
public class ReportService {
}
```

Bean application startup par immediately create nahi hoga; first time required hone par initialize hoga.

Useful when:

* expensive bean initialization
* rarely used functionality
* startup time reduce karna ho

---

# 4. Proper Bean Scope choose karo

Default Spring scope:

```text
Singleton
```

Agar bean stateless hai to singleton generally suitable hai.

Other scopes:

```text
Singleton
Prototype
Request
Session
Application
```

Unnecessarily prototype/request/session beans create karne se resource usage increase ho sakta hai.

---

# 5. Same type ke multiple beans ho to `@Qualifier`

Suppose:

```java
@Bean
public PaymentService cardPayment() {
    return new CardPaymentService();
}

@Bean
public PaymentService upiPayment() {
    return new UpiPaymentService();
}
```

Ab Spring ko confusion ho sakta hai:

```text
PaymentService
     ↓
Which bean?
Card?
UPI?
```

`@Qualifier` use kar sakte hain:

```java
@Autowired
@Qualifier("upiPayment")
private PaymentService paymentService;
```

---

# 6. Conditional Beans

Har environment me har bean required nahi hota.

Spring Boot me:

```java
@ConditionalOnProperty(
    name = "payment.enabled",
    havingValue = "true"
)
@Bean
public PaymentService paymentService() {
    return new PaymentService();
}
```

Agar property enabled nahi hai to bean create nahi hoga.

---

# 7. Profiles

Different environments ke liye:

```text
dev
qa
uat
prod
```

Example:

```java
@Profile("prod")
@Bean
public ProductionService productionService() {
    return new ProductionService();
}
```

Isse unnecessary environment-specific beans load nahi honge.

---

# 🎯 Interview Answer

> **“If the number of beans increases significantly, I would organize them into modular configuration and package structures and avoid creating unnecessary beans. I would use appropriate bean scopes, `@Lazy` for expensive beans that are not immediately required, `@Qualifier` when multiple beans of the same type exist, and `@Profile` or conditional configuration for environment-specific beans. This keeps the application maintainable and avoids unnecessary resource consumption.”**

### 🧠 Memory Trick

**Many Beans →**

```text
Organize
   ↓
Avoid unnecessary beans
   ↓
Proper Scope
   ↓
@Lazy
   ↓
@Qualifier
   ↓
@Profile / @Conditional
```
