# 🆕 KARAT ACTUAL QUESTION — Q20

### Question:

**How do you mock a repository and verify interactions?**

Is question ka main concept hai **Mockito**.

Suppose `UserService` internally `UserRepository` ko call karta hai. Unit test me hum **actual database nahi chalana chahte**, isliye repository ko mock karte hain.

---

## 1. Service

```java
@Service
public class UserService {

    private final UserRepository repository;

    public UserService(UserRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    public User getUser(Long id) {
        return repository.findById(id)
                .orElseThrow();
    }
}
```

---

## 2. Repository ko mock karo

```java
@Mock
private UserRepository repository;
```

Service ko mocked repository provide karo:

```java
@InjectMocks
private UserService userService;
```

Mockito:

```text
@Mock
    ↓
Fake repository

@InjectMocks
    ↓
UserService me fake repository inject
```

---

## 3. Repository ka behavior define karo

```java
when(repository.findById(1L))
        .thenReturn(Optional.of(user));
```

Meaning:

> Jab `findById(1L)` call ho, actual DB me mat jao; ye `user` return karo.

---

## 4. Method test karo

```java
User result = userService.getUser(1L);

assertEquals("Vishnu", result.getName());
```

---

## 5. Verify karo ki repository call hua

```java
verify(repository).findById(1L);
```

Iska matlab:

> Check karo ki `repository.findById(1L)` actually call hua tha.

Specific number of times bhi check kar sakte ho:

```java
verify(repository, times(1))
        .findById(1L);
```

Agar ensure karna hai ki call nahi hua:

```java
verify(repository, never())
        .deleteById(anyLong());
```

---

# Complete Test

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class UserServiceTest {

    @Mock
    private UserRepository repository;

    @InjectMocks
    private UserService userService;

    @Test
    void shouldGetUser() {

        User user = new User(1L, "Vishnu");

        when(repository.findById(1L))
                .thenReturn(Optional.of(user));

        User result = userService.getUser(1L);

        assertEquals("Vishnu", result.getName());

        verify(repository, times(1))
                .findById(1L);
    }
}
```

---

# `when()` vs `verify()`

Ye interview me important hai:

### `when()`

**Behavior define karta hai.**

```java
when(repository.findById(1L))
        .thenReturn(Optional.of(user));
```

> "Agar ye call aaye to ye result dena."

### `verify()`

**Interaction check karta hai.**

```java
verify(repository).findById(1L);
```

> "Kya ye method call hua?"

---

## 🎯 Interview Answer

> **"I use Mockito to mock the repository so that the unit test does not access the actual database. Using `when()` I define the expected repository behavior, and using `verify()` I check whether the repository method was called with the expected arguments and number of times. This allows me to test the service layer in isolation."**

### 🧠 Memory Trick

**`@Mock` → Fake dependency**

**`when()` → Define behavior**

**`@InjectMocks` → Inject fake dependency**

**`verify()` → Check interaction**

