## 🔥 KARAT ACTUAL QUESTION — Q27

### **How do you implement Correlation IDs and Distributed Tracing in Microservices?**

Imagine:

```text
Client
  ↓
API Gateway
  ↓
Order Service
  ↓
Payment Service
  ↓
Notification Service
```

One request goes through **multiple services**. If something fails, we need to identify:

> "Ye logs kis same request ke hain?"

That's where **Correlation ID** helps.

---

## 1️⃣ Correlation ID

A unique ID is generated for a request:

```text
Correlation-ID = abc-123
```

Then the same ID is passed through all services:

```text
Client
  ↓ Correlation-ID: abc-123
API Gateway
  ↓ abc-123
Order Service
  ↓ abc-123
Payment Service
  ↓ abc-123
Notification Service
```

So Elasticsearch/Kibana logs can be searched using:

```text
abc-123
```

and we can see the complete request flow.

### Example log

```text
2026-09-27 INFO [correlationId=abc-123] Order created
2026-09-27 INFO [correlationId=abc-123] Payment initiated
2026-09-27 ERROR [correlationId=abc-123] Payment failed
```

This is especially useful in the ELK setup you have worked with.

---

# 2️⃣ How to implement?

In Spring Boot, commonly use **MDC** for putting the correlation ID into logs.

Example:

```java
MDC.put("correlationId", correlationId);
```

Then Logback pattern:

```text
[%X{correlationId}] %msg%n
```

Log becomes:

```text
[abc-123] Payment initiated
```

For incoming requests, you can use a filter:

```java
@Component
public class CorrelationIdFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(
            HttpServletRequest request,
            HttpServletResponse response,
            FilterChain filterChain) throws ServletException, IOException {

        String correlationId = request.getHeader("X-Correlation-ID");

        if (correlationId == null) {
            correlationId = UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        }

        MDC.put("correlationId", correlationId);

        try {
            filterChain.doFilter(request, response);
        } finally {
            MDC.remove("correlationId");
        }
    }
}
```

---

# 3️⃣ Distributed Tracing

Correlation ID tells us:

> **Which request?**

Distributed tracing tells us:

> **Where did the request go and how much time did each service take?**

Example:

```text
Trace ID: T100

API Gateway       100 ms
     ↓
Order Service     200 ms
     ↓
Payment Service   800 ms
     ↓
Notification      100 ms
```

We can see that **Payment Service took 800 ms**.

---

## Correlation ID vs Distributed Tracing

| Correlation ID                     | Distributed Tracing            |
| ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Identifies request across services | Shows complete request journey |
| Mainly useful for logs             | Useful for logs + performance  |
| Search logs using ID               | Trace spans/services/timing    |
| Simple concept                     | More detailed                  |

### Memory Trick 🧠

> **Correlation ID = "Which request?"**
> **Trace = "Where did request go?"**
> **Span = "What did each service do?"**

### 🎯 Interview Answer

> "In microservices, I use a correlation ID to identify the same request across multiple services. The ID is propagated through HTTP headers and added to MDC so it appears in logs. For distributed tracing, tools such as OpenTelemetry can create a trace with multiple spans, allowing us to see the request flow and latency across services."

