## 🔥 KARAT ACTUAL QUESTION — Q37

### **What is the difference between `@Async` and `CompletableFuture` in Spring Boot?**

This is a common confusion.

### `@Async`

`@Async` tells Spring:

> **"Is method ko calling thread mein execute mat karo; Spring ke async executor/thread pool mein execute karo."**

Example:

```java
@Service
public class EmailService {

    @Async
    public void sendEmail() {
        System.out.println("Sending email...");
    }
}
```

Caller:

```java
emailService.sendEmail();

System.out.println("Continue...");
```

Flow:

```text
Main Thread
    |
    |----> @Async sendEmail()
    |             |
    |             ↓
    |         Other Thread
    |
    ↓
Continue...
```

So caller unnecessarily wait nahi karta.

---

# `CompletableFuture`

`CompletableFuture` **asynchronous result ko represent aur compose** karta hai.

Example:

```java
@Async
public CompletableFuture<String> getUser() {

    String user = "Vishnu";

    return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(user);
}
```

Ab caller result ke saath chaining kar sakta hai:

```java
getUser()
    .thenApply(String::toUpperCase)
    .thenAccept(System.out::println);
```

Flow:

```text
getUser()
   ↓
CompletableFuture<String>
   ↓
thenApply()
   ↓
thenAccept()
```

---

# 🔥 Main Difference

```text
@Async
   ↓
"Method ko kis thread par execute karna hai?"

CompletableFuture
   ↓
"Async result ko kaise represent/chain/combine karna hai?"
```

### Important:

**`@Async` aur `CompletableFuture` competitors nahi hain.**

Dono ko together use bhi kar sakte ho:

```java
@Async
public CompletableFuture<User> getUser() {
    User user = repository.findUser();
    return CompletableFuture.completedFuture(user);
}
```

Here:

```text
@Async
→ execution asynchronously karwa raha hai

CompletableFuture
→ result ko represent kar raha hai
```

---

## ⚠️ Important Spring point

`@Async` generally **Spring proxy** ke through kaam karta hai.

Agar same class ke andar:

```java
public void methodA() {
    methodB();
}

@Async
public void methodB() {
}
```

`methodA()` se direct `methodB()` call karoge, to `@Async` expected way mein apply nahi ho sakta because **self-invocation Spring proxy ko bypass kar sakti hai**.

Usually async method ko separate Spring bean/service mein rakhna safer approach hai.

---

## 🧠 Interview Memory Trick

> **`@Async` = execution**

> **`CompletableFuture` = result + composition**

### 🎯 Interview Answer

> "`@Async` is a Spring feature that executes a method asynchronously using a Spring-managed executor. `CompletableFuture` is a Java API used to represent and compose asynchronous results. They can be used together: `@Async` controls asynchronous execution, while `CompletableFuture` allows us to chain, combine, and handle the result."

