## Q47. Fail-fast vs Weakly-Consistent Iterators?

Tumhare notes ka exact answer:

> **Fail-fast** throw `ConcurrentModificationException` on structural change; **weakly-consistent** tolerate some concurrent changes.

### 1. Fail-fast kya hai?

Fail-fast iterator bolta hai:

> "Agar mere iteration ke time collection structurally modify hua, to main error de dunga."

Example:

```java
List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();

list.add(10);
list.add(20);
list.add(30);

for (Integer x : list) {
    if (x == 20) {
        list.add(40);   // modification
    }
}
```

Yahan generally:

```text
ConcurrentModificationException
```

aa sakta hai.

**Reason:** `ArrayList` ka iterator modification detect karta hai aur iteration ko fail kar deta hai.

---

## 2. Weakly-consistent kya hai?

Weakly-consistent iterator concurrent modification ko tolerate karta hai.

Example:

```java
ConcurrentHashMap<Integer, String> map =
        new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

map.put(1, "A");
map.put(2, "B");
map.put(3, "C");

for (Integer key : map.keySet()) {

    if (key == 2) {
        map.put(4, "D");
    }
}
```

Yahan iterator normally `ConcurrentModificationException` throw nahi karta.

Lekin important point:

**Iterator guarantee nahi karta ki modification ke baad ka har element iteration me zaroor dikhega.**

Isliye naam hai:

**Weakly Consistent**

---

## Simple difference

| Fail-fast                                        | Weakly-consistent                          |
| ------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------ |
| Modification detect hone par fail ho sakta hai   | Concurrent modification tolerate karta hai |
| `ConcurrentModificationException` aa sakta hai   | Normally CME nahi                          |
| `ArrayList`, `HashMap` iterators                 | `ConcurrentHashMap` iterators              |
| Safer against unexpected structural modification | Concurrent access ke liye designed         |

### Interview answer 🎯

> **Fail-fast iterators detect structural modifications during iteration and may throw `ConcurrentModificationException`. Weakly-consistent iterators, such as those of `ConcurrentHashMap`, tolerate concurrent modifications and don't guarantee that every modification will be visible during the iteration.**

### Ek important clarification

**Fail-fast ka matlab thread-safe nahi hota.**
Aur **weakly-consistent ka matlab snapshot bhi nahi hota.**

Ye distinction interview me important hai.

