## Q48. Unmodifiable vs Immutable

### Interview Question

**What is the difference between Unmodifiable and Immutable?**

### Short Interview Answer

> **Unmodifiable** collection ka matlab hai ki us particular reference/view ke through collection ko modify nahi kar sakte, but underlying collection kisi aur reference se change ho sakti hai.
> **Immutable** object/collection ko banane ke baad uska state change hi nahi ho sakta.

---

# 1. Unmodifiable kya hai?

Maan lo hamare paas original list hai:

```java
List<String> original = new ArrayList<>();

original.add("Java");
original.add("Spring");
```

Ab hum uski unmodifiable view banate hain:

```java
List<String> view = Collections.unmodifiableList(original);
```

Ab:

```java
view.add("Kafka");
```

❌ Exception:

```text
UnsupportedOperationException
```

Lekin important point ye hai:

```java
original.add("Kafka");
```

Ye allowed hai.

Aur:

```java
System.out.println(view);
```

Output:

```text
[Java, Spring, Kafka]
```

### Why?

Because `view` **original list ki view** hai.

Diagram:

```text
original
   |
   |----> [Java, Spring]
   |
   ↓
 unmodifiable view
```

`view` se modification allowed nahi hai.

But `original` se modification allowed hai.

Isliye original change hoga to `view` bhi updated data dikha sakti hai.

---

# 2. Immutable kya hai?

Immutable ka matlab:

> Object create hone ke baad uska internal state change nahi ho sakta.

Example:

```java
String name = "Java";
```

Agar:

```java
name = name.concat(" Spring");
```

to existing `"Java"` object change nahi hua.

Instead new String object create hua.

Conceptually:

```text
"Java"
   ↓
  OLD OBJECT
   ↓
never changes

"Java Spring"
   ↓
NEW OBJECT
```

Isi wajah se `String` immutable hai.

---

# 3. Collection example

Java mein:

```java
List<String> list = List.of("Java", "Spring");
```

`List.of()` se bani list immutable/unmodifiable collection semantics provide karti hai.

```java
list.add("Kafka");
```

❌

```text
UnsupportedOperationException
```

Aur iske peeche koi separate mutable `ArrayList` nahi hai jise aap kisi doosre reference se modify kar rahe ho.

---

# 4. Sabse important difference

Ye example interview mein yaad rakho:

### Unmodifiable

```java
List<String> original = new ArrayList<>();

original.add("Java");

List<String> view =
        Collections.unmodifiableList(original);

original.add("Spring");

System.out.println(view);
```

Output:

```text
[Java, Spring]
```

Because:

```text
original  → mutable
view      → modification through view blocked
```

---

### Immutable

Conceptually:

```java
List<String> list =
        List.of("Java", "Spring");
```

Ab list ko change nahi kar sakte.

```text
list
 ↓
[Java, Spring]

No other mutable backing list
        ↓
State cannot be changed
```

---

# 5. Ek real-life example

### Unmodifiable = Glass window

Socho ek shop ka glass window hai.

Tum glass ke through andar ka samaan dekh sakte ho:

```text
          SHOP
   [ Java, Spring ]

       ↑
     Window
```

Window se tum items change nahi kar sakte.

But shopkeeper andar se item change kar sakta hai.

```text
Shopkeeper
    ↓
Original collection changes

Window
    ↓
New state visible
```

---

### Immutable = Printed photograph

Photo print ho gaya:

```text
[ Java | Spring ]
```

Ab us printed photo ke andar ka content change nahi kar sakte.

Agar new content chahiye:

```text
OLD PHOTO
    ↓
NEW PHOTO
```

---

# 6. Interview mein exact answer

> **Unmodifiable is a restriction on a particular reference or view. The underlying collection can still be modified through another reference. Immutable means the object's state itself cannot be changed after construction.**

### One-line trick:

```text
Unmodifiable → "Is reference se change nahi kar sakte."

Immutable    → "Object ko hi change nahi kar sakte."
```

### Q48 ka key point

```text
Collections.unmodifiableList(original)
            ↓
        VIEW
            ↓
original can still change

List.of(...)
    ↓
immutable-style collection
    ↓
cannot modify
```

