## 🔥 Q52. How do you safely delete elements while iterating over a collection?

Ye tumhare image wale **Q52** ka question hai.

### Problem

Maan lo:

```java
List<Integer> numbers =
        new ArrayList<>(List.of(10, 20, 30, 40));
```

Hume `20` remove karna hai.

Agar directly loop mein:

```java
for (Integer n : numbers) {
    if (n == 20) {
        numbers.remove(n);
    }
}
```

❌ Problem aa sakti hai:

```text
ConcurrentModificationException
```

---

# ✅ Solution 1: Iterator use karo

```java
Iterator<Integer> iterator = numbers.iterator();

while (iterator.hasNext()) {

    Integer n = iterator.next();

    if (n == 20) {
        iterator.remove();
    }
}
```

Yahan:

```java
iterator.remove();
```

**safe removal method** hai.

---

# ✅ Solution 2: `removeIf()`

Modern Java mein simple way:

```java
numbers.removeIf(n -> n == 20);
```

Result:

```text
[10, 30, 40]
```

Agar condition complex ho:

```java
numbers.removeIf(n -> n % 2 == 0);
```

to saare even numbers remove ho jayenge.

---

# ❌ Ye mat karo

```java
for (Integer n : numbers) {
    numbers.remove(n);
}
```

Because enhanced `for` loop internally iterator use karta hai, aur collection ko directly modify karna iterator ke expected modification tracking ko break kar sakta hai.

---

# 🧠 Easy Rule

```text
Loop ke andar remove karna hai?
          ↓
   Iterator.remove()
          OR
      removeIf()
```

### 🎯 Interview Answer

> **"When removing elements during iteration, I avoid modifying the collection directly. For an iterator-based loop, I use `Iterator.remove()`. In modern Java, when the condition can be expressed as a predicate, `removeIf()` is usually the simplest option."**

