## 🔥 Q58. What is Method Security in Spring Security?

Ye tumhare image wale **Q58** ka question hai.

### Method Security kya hai?

Simple language mein:

> **Method Security ka matlab hai kisi particular Java method ko role/permission ke basis par protect karna.**

Normal URL security:

```text
/user/** → USER
/admin/** → ADMIN
```

Method security mein hum directly **method ke upar** security lagate hain.

---

## 1️⃣ `@PreAuthorize`

Sabse common example:

```java
@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
public void deleteUser(Long id) {
    // delete user
}
```

Meaning:

```text
Method call
    ↓
User ke paas ADMIN role hai?
    ↓
YES → method execute ✅
NO  → Access Denied ❌
```

---

## 2️⃣ Enable Method Security

Spring Boot mein:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableMethodSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {
}
```

Ab method-level annotations use kar sakte ho.

---

## 3️⃣ `hasRole()` example

```java
@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
public void deleteUser(Long id) {
    userRepository.deleteById(id);
}
```

ADMIN:

```text
deleteUser()
     ↓
Allowed ✅
```

USER:

```text
deleteUser()
     ↓
Access Denied ❌
```

---

## 4️⃣ `hasAuthority()`

Role ke alawa specific authority bhi check kar sakte ho:

```java
@PreAuthorize("hasAuthority('USER_DELETE')")
public void deleteUser(Long id) {
}
```

Meaning:

> User ke paas `USER_DELETE` permission honi chahiye.

---

## 5️⃣ `@PostAuthorize`

`@PreAuthorize` **method execute hone se pehle** check karta hai.

`@PostAuthorize` **method execute hone ke baad** returned result ke basis par authorization check kar sakta hai.

Example:

```java
@PostAuthorize("returnObject.owner == authentication.name")
public Account getAccount(Long id) {
    return accountService.findById(id);
}
```

Flow:

```text
Request
  ↓
Method executes
  ↓
Result returned
  ↓
Authorization check
  ↓
Allowed / Denied
```

---

# 🔥 `@PreAuthorize` vs `@PostAuthorize`

| `@PreAuthorize`                   | `@PostAuthorize`             |
| --------------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Method se pehle check             | Method ke baad check         |
| Input/authentication based checks | Returned result based checks |
| Commonly used                     | More specific use cases      |

### 🧠 Easy Trick

```text
PRE  → Pehle check
POST → Baad mein check
```

---

# 🎯 Interview Answer

> **"Method security allows us to apply authorization rules directly to service or controller methods. In Spring Security, we can enable it using `@EnableMethodSecurity` and use annotations such as `@PreAuthorize` and `@PostAuthorize` to control access based on roles or authorities."**

### Real-world example

```java
@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
public void deleteCustomer(Long id) {
    // delete customer
}
```

Yaani **URL access mil gaya iska matlab ye nahi ki method automatically sabke liye allowed hai**. Method level par bhi authorization enforce kar sakte hain.

